# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Structure et style
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_FR, topic="Structure et style",
    title="Structure et style : le vers, la scène et le récit",
    slug="fru1-structure-style-francais",
    minutes=25,
    summary="How to describe and judge the structure and style of a poem (alexandrine, rhyme, sonnet), a comedy (exposition to dénouement, kinds of comic) and a first-person narrative (narration, description, flash-back), and how to show a technique is effective.",
    objectives=[
        "Scan an alexandrine, applying the rules for the e muet, and name rhyme schemes",
        "Describe the structure of a play from exposition to dénouement and name the kinds of comic",
        "Define narration, description and flash-back and explain what each does for the reader",
        "Write a Point-Example-Effect paragraph that shows how effective a technique is",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Alexandrine", "Césure", "Structure externe et interne", "Exposition et dénouement", "Flash-back (analepse)", "Figure de style"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When the Board's report describes a question on techniques in Hugo's novel, it says what candidates were expected to do: say what narration, description and flash-back are, show why such devices are useful in a work, and then use the text to show how effective they are. Most candidates stopped after giving examples. This lesson gives you the words and the method for the three genres."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("Poetry: structure externe, structure interne"),
        D("The **structure externe** of a poem is its physical shape: number of strophes, number of syllables per line, rhyme scheme. The **structure interne** is the movement of meaning from one part to the next. The Board's report on the unseen-poem question asks for both.", title="Définitions"),
        D("An **alexandrine** is a line of twelve syllables with a pause (the **césure**) after the sixth. In counting, an **e muet** counts as a syllable before a consonant (« la lune brille » : la lu-ne bril-le) but not before a vowel (it elides) and not at the end of a line.", title="Compter les syllabes"),
        EX("Worked example 1: scanning two invented lines", intro="Scan these two invented alexandrines: « Le soleil se levait sur le fleuve endormi, / Et les barques dormaient près du quai assoupi. »", steps=[
            S("Line 1: syllables", text="Le(1) so(2) leil(3) se(4) le(5) vait(6) | sur(7) le(8) fleu(9) ven(10) dor(11) mi(12). The e muet of « fleuve » elides before « en », so « fleuve endormi » gives fleu-ven-dor-mi."),
            S("Line 2: syllables", text="Et(1) les(2) bar(3) ques(4) dor(5) maient(6) | près(7) du(8) quai(9) as(10) sou(11) pi(12). The e of « barques » counts because « dormaient » begins with a consonant. The ending -aient of « dormaient » is not an e muet: it counts as one syllable."),
            S("Rhyme", text="endormi / assoupi: two rhyming masculine endings, so these two lines are a **rime plate** couple (AA)."),
            S("Césure", text="After « levait » and after « dormaient » the sense pauses: 6 + 6, a balanced rhythm that suits a calm scene."),
        ]),
        IMG("alfru1-alexandrin.svg", alt="The invented alexandrine Le soleil se levait sur le fleuve endormi divided into twelve numbered syllables with a pause after the sixth.", caption="Figure 1. An invented alexandrine: twelve syllables, césure after the sixth."),
        KEY("Rhymes and forms", "Three rhyme schemes and one fixed form you must recognise.", cards=[
            ["Rimes plates", "AABB: two lines rhyme, then the next two."],
            ["Rimes croisées", "ABAB: the rhymes alternate."],
            ["Rimes embrassées", "ABBA: one rhyme is enclosed by another."],
            ["Le sonnet", "Fourteen lines: two quatrains then two tercets. Many of Baudelaire's poems are sonnets (for example « Correspondances »). Check your own selection."],
        ]),
        T("Rhymes are either **masculines** (ending in a sounded syllable) or **féminines** (ending in an unsounded e). In the classical tradition they alternate. Always link form to meaning: a short, broken line may suit anguish, a long, even line a calm picture."),
        H("Theatre: the shape of a comedy"),
        KEY("Une structure dramatique", "A play moves through stages. Name them in your essay and say where they fall in your text.", cards=[
            ["Exposition", "We learn who the characters are, where and when, and what each wants."],
            ["Nœud et péripéties", "The knot forms and complications follow: secrets, misunderstandings, tricks."],
            ["Dénouement", "The knot is untied: the situation is resolved."],
        ]),
        T("*Les Fausses Confidences* has three acts. Use your copy to say what each act does (for example, which act sets Dorante in Araminte's house and which sees the scheme tested). Look for **comique**: de mots (wit, wordplay), de situation (hidden things, misunderstandings), de caractère (a person's flaws such as Arlequin's naïveté). Notice also **dramatic irony**: the audience knows more than Araminte does about the scheme, and so watches with a double pleasure. Marivaux's elegant and subtle talk of love has given a word to the language, *marivaudage*, which is worth knowing."),
        H("Prose: narration, description, flash-back"),
        D("**Narration** tells events in order. **Description** pauses to show a place, a person or an object. A **flash-back** (retour en arrière, or analepse) interrupts the present of the story to return to the past.", title="Définitions"),
        T("In *Le Dernier Jour d'un condamné* the narrator speaks in the first person from within his prison. His memories of his earlier life, his family, his young daughter, are flash-backs set against a present that is shrinking. Check in your copy how the memories are triggered and what they do. The descriptions of cells, courtyards and faces are not decoration: they show how the condemned man sees the world."),
        KEY("What each technique does", "Link each technique to an effect on the reader.", cards=[
            ["Narration (first person)", "Puts the reader inside the narrator's thoughts and fears."],
            ["Description", "Builds the setting and reveals the narrator's state of mind through what he notices."],
            ["Flash-back", "Gives depth to the character, creates contrast with the present and increases sympathy."],
        ]),
        H("Style: figures and effects"),
        KEY("Figures to know", "Name the figure, quote the words, say the effect.", cards=[
            ["Comparaison / métaphore", "« Ses yeux, deux lacs calmes » (metaphor, invented). Creates an image."],
            ["Antithèse", "Opposes two ideas: « la lumière et l'ombre »."],
            ["Anaphore", "Repetition at the start of lines: it insists, it builds rhythm."],
            ["Gradation", "Increasing intensity: « il hésita, il trembla, il pleura »."],
            ["Hyperbole / euphémisme", "Exaggeration or softening of the expression."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: showing a technique is effective", intro="Invented question: « Montrez que le flash-back est efficace dans un récit à la première personne. » Write one paragraph using Point, Example, Effect.", steps=[
            S("Point", text="« Le flash-back permet au narrateur de comparer sa vie passée à sa situation présente. »"),
            S("Example", text="Choose from your own copy a memory that interrupts the present and say where it begins and ends. (Use a short reference, not a long quotation.)"),
            S("Effect", text="« Ce contraste augmente la souffrance du narrateur et la compassion du lecteur. »"),
            S("Link", text="« Le flash-back est donc efficace parce qu'il révèle le caractère du personnage et renforce l'émotion. »"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Counting an e muet at the end of a line", "The final e of a line is not counted. In « légère » at the end of a line, count lé-gè and stop. Count an e muet only inside the line, before a consonant."),
        MISTAKE("Naming without judging", "« Il y a une métaphore » earns little. Write: « La métaphore X montre Y et provoque Z chez le lecteur. » The Board's report says candidates listed examples without evaluating how effective they are."),
        TIP("Exam link: the unseen-poem question in Paper 2 expects both external and internal structure and a clear judgement on the poet's attitude; the prose questions ask you to evaluate techniques. Use your own copy for examples and write Point, Example, Effect."),
        qz("How many syllables has the line « Et les barques dormaient près du quai assoupi » (the e of « barques » is before a consonant, the line ends in a sounded vowel)?",
           "Twelve",
           [("Eleven", "You forgot to count the e muet of « barques », which is before a consonant (dormaient)."),
            ("Thirteen", "You counted -aient of « dormaient » twice. It is one syllable."),
            ("Ten", "You left out syllables such as the e of « barques » and the « as » of « assoupi ».")],
           1, "Et-les-bar-ques-dor-maient (6) près-du-quai-as-sou-pi (6): twelve syllables with the césure after « dormaient ».", hint="Count the e of barques."),
        qz("What is a flash-back?",
           "A return to a moment of the past that interrupts the present of the story",
           [("A description of a place", "That is a description, which pauses the action without going back in time."),
            ("The final solution of a play", "That is the dénouement."),
            ("A rhyme pattern ABBA", "That is rimes embrassées.")],
           0, "A flash-back (analepse) leaves the present of the narrative to show an earlier event, often to create contrast or reveal character.", hint="It concerns time, not form."),
        PQ("Scan the invented line « La nuit tombait sans bruit sur le petit village » and say whether it is an alexandrine.", "La(1) nuit(2) tom(3) bait(4) sans(5) bruit(6) | sur(7) le(8) pe(9) tit(10) vil(11) la(12) ge. The e of « le » and of « petit » (pe-tit) count because a consonant follows, and the final e of « village » is not counted at the end of a line. Twelve syllables with a pause after « bruit »: yes, it is an alexandrine with a 6 + 6 rhythm."),
        PQ("Name the three stages of a play's structure and say what Marivaux's audience learns in the first.", "Exposition, nœud (with péripéties) and dénouement. In the exposition the audience learns who Araminte, Dorante and the others are, where the action takes place and what each character wants: for example, Dorante's love for Araminte and his entry into her service as intendant, and Madame Argante's plan for her daughter. Check act and scene references in your copy."),
        PQ("Write one paragraph (Point, Example, Effect) on the effectiveness of description in a prose text of your choice. Use placeholders for the example.", "Point: « La description sert à montrer l'état d'esprit du narrateur. » Example: choose a description in your copy (for example of a place where the character is shut in) and give its chapter or page. Effect: « En choisissant des détails sombres, l'auteur transmet l'angoisse du personnage et fait entrer le lecteur dans sa perception. » Link: « La description est donc efficace parce qu'elle ne fait pas que décrire un lieu: elle révèle un être. »"),
        SUM(
            "In poetry, study structure externe (strophes, syllables, rhymes) and structure interne (movement of meaning); an alexandrine has twelve syllables with a césure after the sixth.",
            "Count an e muet before a consonant, elide it before a vowel and ignore it at the end of the line.",
            "A play moves from exposition through nœud and péripéties to dénouement, and a comedy plays with comique de mots, de situation and de caractère.",
            "In prose, narration, description and flash-back each have an effect; always show how effective they are, using Point, Example, Effect.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. Lire l'oeuvre africaine au programme
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_AF, topic="Lire l'œuvre africaine au programme",
    title="Lire l'œuvre africaine au programme : contexte, lecture et fiche",
    slug="fru1-lire-oeuvre-africaine",
    minutes=22,
    summary="How to prepare a prescribed Francophone work: place it in its literary and historical context (négritude, independence), read it for society as well as story, and build a fiche for Bebey, Césaire and Nkoa Atenga from your own copy.",
    objectives=[
        "Place Francophone literature in context: négritude, the independence period and the oral tradition",
        "Name the Francophone works the Board's 2023 report mentions and say which genre each belongs to",
        "Read a Francophone text for society (family, authority, power) as well as for plot",
        "Build a fiche de lecture for a Francophone work, and avoid confusing a Caribbean author with an African one",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Littérature francophone", "Négritude", "Contexte historique", "Tradition orale", "Fiche de lecture"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In a village near Douala, an evening of storytelling and a family meeting shape how people speak about marriage, respect and duty. Francophone African literature of the 1960s often starts from such scenes. If you read Francis Bebey's novel only as a love story, you miss half of it. If you read it with the family, the elders and the community in mind, the text opens up. This lesson shows how to read the Francophone works of your programme with context in mind."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("Which works, which genres?"),
        T("The blueprint says only that this module treats « the prescribed Cameroonian/African Francophone literary texts ». The Board's 2023 report names three works in Paper 2: **Aimé Césaire**, *La Tragédie du roi Christophe* (drama); **Nkoa Atenga**, *L'Enfant de la révolte muette* (prose); **Francis Bebey**, *Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio* (prose). **Check your school's list**: the prescribed works change from one syllabus to another."),
        D("**La littérature francophone** is literature written in French by authors outside France (Africa, the Caribbean, Canada, and so on). It is not the same as « littérature française », and it often explores colonial history, cultural identity and the clash between tradition and modernity.", title="Définition"),
        KEY("Be precise about each author", "Do not label every author « African » without checking.", cards=[
            ["Francis Bebey (1929–2001)", "Born in Douala, Cameroon. Writer and musician. *Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio* (1967)."],
            ["Aimé Césaire (1913–2008)", "Born in Martinique (Caribbean), a founder of négritude, a poet, playwright and politician. His play about Haiti's King Christophe appeared in 1963. He is Francophone but not African: say so in your essays."],
            ["Nkoa Atenga", "The report names *L'Enfant de la révolte muette*. Find in your own copy the author's details, the date and the setting."],
        ]),
        H("Context: négritude, independence, orality"),
        T("**Négritude** is a literary and cultural movement of the 1930s that Césaire, Léopold Sédar Senghor and Léon-Gontran Damas are known for. It affirmed the dignity and cultural value of Black peoples against colonial denigration. Césaire's long poem *Cahier d'un retour au pays natal* (1939) and his essay *Discours sur le colonialisme* (1950) are landmarks. In the 1960s, many African countries gained independence (Cameroon's French-administered part on 1 January 1960). Writers then explored the problems of new states and of changing societies. Bebey's novel (1967) belongs to this period."),
        IMG("alfru1-timeline-francophone.svg", alt="A timeline from the 1930s to 1967 with négritude in the 1930s, Césaire's Cahier in 1939, his Discours in 1950, Cameroon's independence in 1960, Césaire's Christophe in 1963 and Bebey's novel in 1967.", caption="Figure 1. A short timeline for your Francophone works (dates from your copy's publication details: check them)."),
        T("Another context: the **oral tradition**. In many African societies stories, proverbs and songs pass between generations through speech. Written texts often borrow this: a narrator who addresses the reader, proverbs, repetition, humour, the voice of a village chorus. Note these when you find them."),
        H("Reading for society"),
        KEY("What to note in a Francophone text", "Five contexts to keep in the margin of your copy.", cards=[
            ["Setting", "Where and when: a village, a city, a royal court. Write the place names."],
            ["Authority", "Who decides: parents, elders, chiefs, a king, a husband? On what grounds?"],
            ["Customs", "Marriage, dowry, funerals, respect for age. Note the words that name them."],
            ["Change", "What changes: school, work, religion, new ideas about women and men?"],
            ["Language", "French mixed with local words, proverbs, humour, irony."],
        ]),
        T("The Board's report gives a precise warning. In a question on parental authority in Bebey's novel, many candidates did not understand that « parents » can be extended to the **elders of the village**, so their work was sketchy. In African societies, family authority is often wide: uncles, aunts and elders may take part in decisions. Read the text with that wider family in mind."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a fiche for Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio", intro="Fill the six-part fiche from well-known facts, then check each against your copy.", steps=[
            S("Identité", text="Francis Bebey, 1967, a novel narrated in the first person by the young Mbenda. Setting: a fishing community in the Douala area (check the place names in your copy)."),
            S("Résumé (your words)", text="Mbenda is pressed by his family to marry Fanny, while he is attracted to Agatha Moudio. Write the main events in about ten lines from your reading."),
            S("Personnages", text="Mbenda (narrator), Fanny, Agatha Moudio and the older relatives and neighbours who give advice. For each, write what he or she wants."),
            S("Thèmes", text="Family and community authority, arranged marriage and personal choice, tradition and change. Add act or chapter references."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a fiche for La Tragédie du roi Christophe", intro="Fill the identity and context of Césaire's play.", steps=[
            S("Identité", text="Aimé Césaire, 1963, a play in three acts about Henri Christophe, who ruled the north of Haiti as king after the Haitian revolution (check the dates in your copy)."),
            S("Context", text="Haiti won its independence from France in 1804 after a revolution led by formerly enslaved people. Christophe tries to build a strong state and prove that Black people can build one. The play asks what such power costs."),
            S("Personnages", text="Christophe, his wife, the courtiers, the archbishop, the rival Pétion who governs the south, Vastey, a writer who serves the king, the rebel Métellus: check the names and roles in your copy."),
            S("Thèmes", text="Power and its limits, nation-building, forced labour, dignity and the memory of slavery, the influence of environment on a leader."),
        ]),
        T("For *L'Enfant de la révolte muette*, only the report's information is certain here: the couple **Nathalie and Jean-Marie** and the questions on equality between women and men. Build your fiche entirely from your own copy: setting, narrator, time, the couple's education and work, family responsibilities, conflicts."),
        MISTAKE("Calling Césaire an African author", "Césaire is a Martinican writer. His play is Francophone and linked to Africa through history and négritude, but if you write « l'auteur africain » you make a factual error. Write « l'auteur martiniquais »."),
        MISTAKE("Using the text as a history book", "A play about Christophe is a literary work, not a history lesson. Use the play for what the author shows and for your argument, and use history only for context."),
        MISTAKE("Ignoring the wider family", "In the African novels, treat « parents » and « elders » as possibly overlapping. Otherwise you miss the full authority that the text shows."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 2 questions on Francophone prose and drama ask you to use the text and often examples from your own society. Prepare quotes from the text and two examples from real life for each major theme. Check the instructions on your own paper."),
        qz("Which statement about Aimé Césaire is accurate?",
           "He is from Martinique and a founder of the négritude movement",
           [("He is a Cameroonian writer from Douala", "Douala is Bebey's birthplace, not Césaire's."),
            ("He was a French novelist who never visited the Caribbean", "He was born in Martinique and wrote about the Caribbean and Africa."),
            ("He wrote Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio", "That novel is by Francis Bebey.")],
           0, "Césaire (1913–2008) was born in Martinique and is one of the founders of négritude. He is Francophone but not African, which matters in an essay.", hint="Where was he born?"),
        qz("According to the Board's 2023 report, why did many candidates give sketchy answers on parental authority in Bebey's novel?",
           "They did not see that « parents » can include the elders of the village",
           [("They did not know that the novel was published in 1967", "The report's concern is about the idea of authority, not the date."),
            ("They wrote about the wrong country", "The report does not say this."),
            ("They thought the novel was a play", "Genre confusion is not what the report says.")],
           3, "The report says candidates did not understand that « parents » could extend to elders, so they treated authority too narrowly.", hint="Think about the extended family."),
        PQ("Write a three-sentence context paragraph for an essay on Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio, placing it in the 1960s.", "Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio, publié en 1967 par le Camerounais Francis Bebey, appartient à la période qui suit les indépendances africaines. À cette époque, les sociétés africaines sont partagées entre les traditions familiales et les idées nouvelles sur le choix personnel et le mariage. Le roman met en scène ces tensions à travers le jeune Mbenda, que sa famille presse de se marier. (Check dates and details in your own copy.)"),
        PQ("List four things you would note in your copy of La Tragédie du roi Christophe for the theme of power.", "1 Who gives orders and how (Christophe, the courtiers) with scene references. 2 What the king says he wants (a strong Black state, proof of capacity) and how he treats workers. 3 How other characters judge him (a rival, a poet-secretary, a rebel). 4 The setting of his power (palace, Citadelle) and how it shapes him. Add act and scene numbers from your copy."),
        PQ("Why is it a mistake to call Césaire « un auteur africain » in an essay, and how would you describe him correctly?", "Because Césaire was born in Martinique, in the Caribbean, so he is not African by birth, even though négritude links him to Africa and his work addresses Black history. In an essay write « l'écrivain martiniquais Aimé Césaire, cofondateur de la négritude » or « l'auteur francophone ». That shows accuracy and awareness of context."),
        SUM(
            "Francophone literature is written in French outside France; place each work in context: négritude, independence and the oral tradition.",
            "The Board's 2023 report names Césaire (drama), Nkoa Atenga and Bebey (prose); check your school's list.",
            "Read for society as well as plot: setting, authority (including elders), customs, change and language.",
            "Build a fiche from your own copy; be exact about each author, and never call Césaire an African-born author.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Themes et contexte (africain)
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_AF, topic="Thèmes et contexte",
    title="Thèmes et contexte : relier le texte à la société",
    slug="fru1-themes-contexte-africain",
    minutes=24,
    summary="How to write the Paper 2 question that asks whether a statement applies in your society and in the text: parental authority, gender equality, and environment and power, with a method that joins real-life examples to exact references.",
    objectives=[
        "Identify the main themes of the Francophone works: authority and community, tradition and individual choice, gender equality, power and environment",
        "Use the method Idée, Exemple de la société, Exemple du texte, Nuance for a statement-and-text question",
        "Show limits as well as support when evaluating a claim",
        "Avoid generalised answers that do not use the text, the weakness the Board's report notes",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Thème", "Contexte social", "Exemple tiré du texte", "Nuance", "Autorité parentale", "Égalité des sexes", "Influence du milieu"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In the Board's report on Paper 2, three of the Francophone questions share a pattern: they give a statement about society, ask whether it applies in your country or locality, and require you to examine it through the text. The statements concerned the influence of environment on political leaders, the saying that what a man can do a woman can do, and parental authority over children. The report says the weakest answers were general and not tied to the text. This lesson gives you a method."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("The main themes"),
        KEY("Themes by work (check against your copy)", "Use these as starting points for your grid, not as facts to repeat.", cards=[
            ["Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio (Bebey)", "Parental and community authority; marriage chosen by the family versus personal choice; tradition and change; the position of a young man between two women and his family."],
            ["L'Enfant de la révolte muette (Nkoa Atenga)", "The couple Nathalie and Jean-Marie and the question of equality between women and men: education, work, responsibility towards the family, fidelity. Add your own copy's themes."],
            ["La Tragédie du roi Christophe (Césaire)", "Power and its limits; building a nation; forced labour; dignity after slavery; how a leader's environment shapes his decisions."],
        ]),
        T("Each theme links text and society. A theme studied only in the book is a literary exercise. A theme linked to examples from your own society becomes an argument, which is what these questions require."),
        H("The method: Idée, Société, Texte, Nuance"),
        D("In a question « Cette idée s'applique-t-elle dans votre société ? Appuyez-vous sur le texte », each body paragraph has four parts: **Idée** (a precise claim), **Société** (an example from real life), **Texte** (an exact example from the work) and **Nuance** (a limit or exception).", title="La méthode ISTN"),
        IMG("alfru1-method-istn.svg", alt="A four-step diagram: Idée, Société, Texte, Nuance, with arrows from each step to the next and a note that the Board's report asks for text-based examples.", caption="Figure 1. The four steps of a paragraph for a statement-and-text question."),
        T("Examples from society should be ordinary and respectful: family decisions, schooling, work, community meetings, local leadership. Avoid party politics and any attack on a group. The examiner wants to see that you understand how the idea works in real life, then how the author shows it."),
        EX("Worked example 1: parental authority", intro="Invented subject for practice: « Dans la société africaine, les parents exercent trop d'autorité sur leurs enfants. Comment le roman de Bebey le montre-t-il ? » Plan two paragraphs.", steps=[
            S("Define the terms", text="« Parents » may include elders and relatives. « Autorité » means the power to decide and to be obeyed. « Trop » invites judgement: too much compared with what?"),
            S("Paragraph 1: Idée and Société", text="Idée: « La famille intervient dans les grandes décisions, comme le mariage. » Société: in many families an older relative is consulted before a marriage or a major choice."),
            S("Paragraph 1: Texte", text="Use one episode from your copy where Mbenda's family insists on a choice for him, with chapter or page, and explain how Mbenda reacts."),
            S("Paragraph 2: Nuance", text="Authority also protects: elders give advice and support. The novel shows both the weight and the care of the group. Conclude with your own judgement."),
        ]),
        H("Gender equality: argue with the text"),
        T("The Board's report says the question on the saying « ce que l'homme peut faire, la femme peut le faire, et même mieux » was the worst-performed in the paper. The reason is stated clearly: candidates could not use facts from the text and gave generalised answers. The expected approach was to point out where equality exists in society and where it is limited, then show how the saying applies to the couple Nathalie and Jean-Marie, comparing domains such as education, work, responsibility towards the family and fidelity."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a comparison grid for a couple", intro="Prepare a grid for the couple in L'Enfant de la révolte muette. Fill it from your own copy.", steps=[
            S("Choose domains", text="Education, work, family responsibility, behaviour in the couple (for example fidelity). The report names these domains."),
            S("Fill the grid", text="For each domain write what Nathalie does, what Jean-Marie does, and a chapter or page reference from your copy."),
            S("Compare", text="Where are they equal? Where is one of them favoured or limited? What does the author seem to think?"),
            S("Conclude", text="Say to what extent the saying is true of the couple, and add a limit: equality in opportunity does not always mean equality in practice."),
        ]),
        H("Environment and power"),
        T("A question on Césaire's play was about how environment influences political actors. The report expected candidates to show that professional, social, economic and political surroundings shape the mindset of leaders, and that some characters' actions are conditioned by their context rather than personal conviction. In the play, think of Christophe's court, the pressure of a new state surrounded by hostile powers, and the demands of building the Citadelle. Link this to examples from your own country about how roles and institutions shape leaders, with respect and without naming parties."),
        KEY("Statement-and-text checklist", "Before you write, tick each item.", cards=[
            ["Terms", "Did I define the key words of the statement?"],
            ["Society", "Do I have a real-life example for each paragraph?"],
            ["Text", "Do I have an exact scene, chapter or page for each paragraph?"],
            ["Nuance", "Have I shown a limit, an exception or a contrary view?"],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Generalised answers", "The Board's report says many candidates could not use facts from the text and so wrote answers with general ideas. Every paragraph needs at least one reference from the work."),
        MISTAKE("Agreeing without testing the claim", "In the report's comment on the Baudelaire question, some candidates simply agreed that poets only write love poems, and so showed no limits. A strong answer shows where the claim applies and where it does not."),
        MISTAKE("Taking sides against a group", "Avoid sweeping statements such as « Les hommes sont toujours... ». Use careful words: « dans certaines familles », « souvent », « dans le texte »."),
        TIP("Exam link: these statement-and-text questions appear in the drama and prose sections of Paper 2. Plan for five minutes, write four or five ISTN paragraphs, and write a brief introduction and conclusion in correct French. Check the instructions on your own paper."),
        qz("Which paragraph would score best for a question on parental authority in Bebey's novel?",
           "A claim about family authority, a real-life example, an exact episode from the text and a limit",
           [("Three pages describing African culture without reference to the text", "The Board's report warns against answers that are general and not linked to the text."),
            ("A summary of the novel from beginning to end", "A summary does not answer the question: you must select and analyse."),
            ("A list of five characters with no commentary", "Names without analysis do not show how authority is exercised.")],
           0, "The ISTN paragraph joins idea, society, text and nuance. The others lack text-based argument or analysis.", hint="Which option contains all four steps?"),
        qz("In the method Idée, Société, Texte, Nuance, what does Nuance mean?",
           "A limit or exception that shows the claim is not true in every case",
           [("Repeating the idea in other words", "That is repetition, not nuance."),
            ("A second quotation from the text", "A quotation is a Texte step, not a limit."),
            ("A personal story with no link to the question", "A personal story that does not relate to the claim does not help.")],
           2, "Nuance shows you can think critically: it recognises where the claim stops being true.", hint="Think of « mais », « cependant »."),
        PQ("Write the Idée and Société steps for a paragraph on this invented claim: « Dans la société, l'environnement façonne le comportement des dirigeants. »", "Idée: « Le milieu professionnel et social dans lequel un dirigeant évolue influence ses choix. » Société: « Dans de nombreuses organisations, un nouveau responsable adopte rapidement les habitudes et les contraintes de ses collègues, des règles de son institution et des attentes de ceux qui l'ont choisi. » Then add Texte (a scene from Césaire's play showing Christophe constrained by his situation) and Nuance (a leader may also resist his environment)."),
        PQ("Give two domains in which you could compare Nathalie and Jean-Marie, and say what you would look for in each.", "Education: what studies each pursued and whether either was limited. Work: what job each has, whether one earns more or is hindered, and how each treats the other's work. Other domains the Board names are responsibility towards the family and fidelity. For each domain, write the chapter or page reference from your own copy."),
        PQ("Explain, in four sentences, why a strong answer on gender equality shows limits to the saying and does not simply agree with it.", "The saying claims that a woman can do what a man can do and even better. An answer that only agrees ignores situations where opportunities or expectations are unequal. A strong answer shows both where equality is real and where it is limited, as the Board's report expects, and ties the discussion to the couple in the text. This shows analysis, not only opinion."),
        SUM(
            "The Francophone questions link a statement about society to the text, so every paragraph needs both a real-life example and an exact reference.",
            "Use the method Idée, Société, Texte, Nuance, and show the limits of the claim.",
            "Themes to prepare: authority and community, gender equality, power and environment; always use careful, respectful language.",
            "The Board's report says generalised answers without facts from the text were the main weakness.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Narration et style (africain)
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_AF, topic="Narration et style",
    title="Narration et style dans les œuvres francophones",
    slug="fru1-narration-style-africain",
    minutes=24,
    summary="How to analyse narration and style in Francophone prose and drama: narrator and point of view, registers, humour and irony, oral features, and stagecraft in Césaire's play, with a model analysis of an invented passage.",
    objectives=[
        "Identify the narrator and the point of view (focalisation) of a prose text",
        "Recognise registers of language, humour, irony and features of orality",
        "Describe how a play uses speech, stage directions and tirades to create effect",
        "Write an analysis that links a technique to its effect on the reader",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Narrateur", "Focalisation", "Registre de langue", "Ironie et humour", "Oralité", "Didascalies", "Tirade"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When you listen to a grandmother tell a story, you hear who is speaking, how she addresses you, when she laughs and when she lowers her voice. Francophone African authors often write the way a storyteller speaks. The Board's report says that Paper 2 questions on prose can ask about techniques of narration, description and flash-back, and about how effective they are. This lesson gives you the tools to analyse narration and style, and to turn observations into arguments."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("Narrator and point of view"),
        D("The **narrateur** is the voice that tells the story. He may be a character (first person: « je ») or outside the story (third person: « il »). The **focalisation** (point of view) says through whose knowledge the story is seen: **interne** (through one character's eyes), **externe** (only outward behaviour, like a camera) or **zéro** (an all-knowing narrator).", title="Définitions"),
        T("In *Le Fils d'Agatha Moudio* the story is told in the first person by the young man Mbenda (check in your copy). A first-person narrator gives intimacy and humour, but he knows only what he has seen or heard: ask yourself what he does **not** understand or hides. For *L'Enfant de la révolte muette*, find in your own copy who tells the story, from what point in time, and whose thoughts the reader can see."),
        KEY("Questions for any prose passage", "Answer these in the margin.", cards=[
            ["Qui parle ?", "A character or an outside narrator? In which person?"],
            ["Qui voit ?", "Through whose eyes is the scene shown (focalisation)?"],
            ["Quel temps ?", "Passé simple, imparfait, présent? Does time jump or return (flash-back)?"],
            ["Quel ton ?", "Serious, comic, ironic, tender, bitter?"],
        ]),
        H("Registers, humour and irony"),
        D("A **registre de langue** is the level of language: **familier** (informal), **courant** (everyday) or **soutenu** (formal). A **registre littéraire** is a tone: comique, ironique, lyrique, pathétique, tragique. **Irony** says one thing to suggest another; **humour** amuses while often hiding a serious point.", title="Définitions"),
        T("Francophone authors often mix registers on purpose. A formal word placed in a casual sentence may create a smile. A village elder's wisdom may be expressed in a proverb, then undercut by the narrator's gentle irony. Ask: **why does the author choose this effect here?**"),
        H("Oralité: the storyteller's voice"),
        KEY("Features of orality in a written text", "Look for these, and quote the exact words.", cards=[
            ["Adresse au lecteur", "The narrator speaks to the listener: « chez nous, on dit que... »"],
            ["Proverbes et images", "A saying or a concrete image replaces an abstract explanation."],
            ["Répétition", "Repeating a phrase for rhythm and emphasis."],
            ["Dialogue vivant", "Short exchanges, interruptions, the voices of the community."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: analysing an invented passage", intro="Analyse this invented passage in the style of a village narrator. « Chez nous, on dit que la parole du vieux est un filet : on ne voit pas ses mailles, mais on ne lui échappe pas. Mon oncle m'avait parlé trois soirs de suite, sans élever la voix. Moi, je regardais mes pieds. Au troisième soir, je connaissais mieux la forme de mes orteils que celle de mon avenir. »", steps=[
            S("Narrator", text="First person (« m'avait », « je »): a young narrator speaking about his uncle. Focalisation interne."),
            S("Orality", text="« Chez nous, on dit que... » introduces collective wisdom; the metaphor of the net (filet) is concrete, like a proverb. « Trois soirs de suite » uses repetition."),
            S("Humour and irony", text="The last sentence compares toes and future: the narrator seems to say that he knows his toes better than his own future, which is comic and suggests he is feeling trapped."),
            S("Effect", text="« La voix familière du narrateur crée de la complicité avec le lecteur, tandis que l'humour exprime sa gêne devant l'autorité de l'oncle. »"),
        ]),
        H("Style in the theatre: Césaire"),
        D("In a play, **didascalies** are stage directions (places, gestures, entrances). A **tirade** is a long speech by one character. A **monologue** is spoken by a character alone on stage. A **dialogue** is an exchange between characters.", title="Définitions"),
        T("Césaire wrote *La Tragédie du roi Christophe* for the stage. As you read, notice how the king speaks (his tirades, his orders), how other characters speak to him, and how the text moves between solemn language, popular scenes and ironic effects. Césaire the poet uses vivid images, often drawn from nature, in his dramatic language. **Check your own edition** for songs, crowd scenes and stage directions. Always say what each effect shows about power, fear or hope."),
        EX("Worked example 2: effectiveness of narration", intro="Invented question: « Montrez que la narration à la première personne est efficace dans un roman africain que vous avez étudié. » Plan one paragraph.", steps=[
            S("Point", text="« La narration à la première personne rend le récit plus proche du lecteur. »"),
            S("Example", text="Select from your copy a passage in which the narrator expresses a feeling or a doubt directly, with its chapter or page, and name the techniques: address to the reader, humour, direct thoughts."),
            S("Effect", text="« Le lecteur partage le point de vue du jeune narrateur, rit avec lui, et comprend l'autorité de la famille de l'intérieur. »"),
            S("Limit", text="« Ce point de vue est limité : le narrateur ne sait pas tout, ce qui laisse place à l'ironie. »"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling every narrator « omniscient »", "A first-person narrator is not all-knowing: he tells what he has lived or been told. Use « narrateur-personnage » and say what he cannot know."),
        MISTAKE("Spotting a feature and stopping", "Writing « il y a de l'humour » is incomplete. Quote the words, name the effect (a smile, a criticism, a warmth) and say why it matters for the theme."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up text and stage", "In a play, the audience sees and hears, so write « le spectateur » as well as « le lecteur » and use the word didascalies for stage directions."),
        TIP("Exam link: prose questions in Paper 2 can ask about narration, description or flash-back. Define the technique, say what it does in general, then show with exact examples how effective it is in your set text. Check the instructions on your own paper."),
        qz("In a first-person narrative the narrator is a young villager. Which statement is correct?",
           "He is a narrator-character and may not understand everything he describes",
           [("He is omniscient and knows every character's thoughts", "That describes focalisation zéro, which is not the case for a first-person character."),
            ("He is the author of the book in every case", "Narrator and author must be distinguished: the narrator is a created voice."),
            ("He cannot use humour", "A first-person narrator can use humour and irony, often to great effect.")],
           0, "A first-person narrator is a narrator-character: he tells what he has seen or heard and can be mistaken, which allows irony.", hint="What does a character know?"),
        qz("The phrase « Chez nous, on dit que... » at the start of a narrator's passage is most clearly a sign of",
           "orality: the narrator speaks like a storyteller to his audience",
           [("a flash-back to a previous chapter", "A flash-back returns to past events, which is not signalled here."),
            ("an alexandrine line", "This is prose, with no metre."),
            ("a stage direction", "Stage directions belong to a play and give directions to actors.")],
           1, "The phrase addresses an audience and cites collective wisdom, typical of an oral storytelling voice.", hint="Who is being addressed?"),
        PQ("Define focalisation interne, externe and zéro in one sentence each, with a tiny example.", "Interne: the story is seen through one character's mind (« Je sentis la peur monter »). Externe: only visible actions are told, not thoughts (« Il se leva, regarda la porte, sortit »). Zéro: an all-knowing narrator who sees inside several characters and outside events (« Elle ignorait que son frère, au même moment, préparait son départ »)."),
        PQ("Write a six-line analysis of this invented sentence: « Mon père parlait peu, mais chaque mot tombait comme une pierre dans l'eau calme. »", "First person (« mon père ») indicates a narrator-character. The comparison « comme une pierre dans l'eau calme » is a simile (comparaison) that gives a concrete image of the father's words: each word is heavy and creates ripples. The contrast between « peu » and « chaque mot » (antithesis) shows that few words have great weight. The effect is to suggest the father's authority and the respect he commands. The sentence uses the imparfait (parlait, tombait) for a habitual, repeated situation. The tone is respectful, with a touch of admiration."),
        PQ("Give three questions you would ask about a play's style before writing an essay on it.", "1 How does each character speak (tirades, short lines, solemn or popular language) and what does it show? 2 What do the didascalies tell the director about places, gestures and entrances? 3 Which images or repeated words build the themes of the play? Use your own edition for examples with act and scene numbers."),
        SUM(
            "Identify the narrator and the point of view: first person gives intimacy but limited knowledge; focalisation interne, externe or zéro tells whose knowledge we share.",
            "Registers, humour and irony are chosen effects; always ask why the author uses them at this point.",
            "Oral features (address to the audience, proverbs, repetition, lively dialogue) give a written text a storyteller's voice.",
            "In a play, analyse tirades, dialogue and didascalies, and always link a technique to its effect.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. La dissertation litteraire
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_DIS, topic="La dissertation littéraire",
    title="La dissertation littéraire : sujet, problématique, plan",
    slug="fru1-dissertation-litteraire",
    minutes=25,
    summary="A step-by-step method for the literary dissertation: analyse the subject, build a problématique, choose a dialectical or thematic plan, write the introduction, paragraphs and conclusion in French, with a model on Les Fausses Confidences.",
    objectives=[
        "Analyse the terms of a literary subject and formulate a problématique",
        "Choose between a dialectical plan (thèse, antithèse, synthèse) and a thematic plan",
        "Write an introduction with amorce, présentation du sujet, problématique and annonce du plan",
        "Write argumentative paragraphs and a conclusion that answers the problem, in correct French",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Dissertation littéraire", "Problématique", "Plan dialectique", "Plan thématique", "Introduction", "Conclusion", "Connecteur logique"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Many Paper 2 questions give a statement and ask you to judge it using your set texts. For example, the Board's report describes questions asking whether poets write only about love, whether a mother always supports her daughter, whether the environment shapes political leaders, or whether women can do what men do. These are not summaries: they are miniature dissertations. A dissertation littéraire is an organised argument on a literary subject, supported by examples from the texts. It has a method, and the method can be learned."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        D("La **dissertation littéraire** est un devoir argumenté qui répond à une question ou discute une affirmation sur la littérature, en s'appuyant sur des œuvres précises. La **problématique** est la question centrale que le sujet pose et à laquelle tout le devoir répond.", title="Définitions"),
        H("Step 1: analyse the subject"),
        T("Underline the key words. Look at the **consigne** (discutez, analysez, montrez que, dans quelle mesure), the **notions** (for instance « intérêt », « mère », « toujours »), and the **limits** (which works? which section?). Words such as « toujours », « seulement » or « trop » invite you to test the claim: is it always true? only true?"),
        KEY("Four questions about any subject", "Answer them on rough paper in two minutes.", cards=[
            ["Que dit le sujet ?", "Reformulate the claim in your own words."],
            ["Que demande la consigne ?", "Discuss, show that, to what extent: each calls for a different movement."],
            ["Quelles œuvres ?", "Which works must you use and how many?"],
            ["Quelle limite ?", "Does the claim hide a word like « toujours » or « seulement » to test?"],
        ]),
        H("Step 2: the problématique and the plan"),
        T("The problématique turns the claim into a question that opens a debate: « Dans quelle mesure... ? » The plan then answers it. Two common plans are shown below. In a **plan dialectique**, the first part supports the claim (thèse), the second shows its limits (antithèse), and the third goes beyond (synthèse). In a **plan thématique**, each part treats an aspect of the subject, for instance one theme per part."),
        IMG("alfru1-plan-dialectique.svg", alt="A diagram of a dialectical plan: introduction leading to three boxes, thèse, antithèse and synthèse, then conclusion.", caption="Figure 1. A dialectical plan: thèse, antithèse, synthèse."),
        EX("Worked example 1: subject, problématique, plan", intro="Invented subject for practice: « Une mère agit toujours dans l'intérêt de sa fille. Discutez à partir de Madame Argante dans Les Fausses Confidences. »", steps=[
            S("Analyse", text="Key words: « toujours » (test it), « intérêt » (whose idea of interest?), « mère » and « fille ». Consigne: discutez, so a balanced answer. One work: Marivaux."),
            S("Problématique", text="« Madame Argante agit-elle dans l'intérêt réel de sa fille, ou seulement selon sa propre idée de cet intérêt ? »"),
            S("Plan dialectique", text="I. Madame Argante agit pour ce qu'elle croit être le bien de sa fille (rang, fortune). II. Mais cette idée entre en conflit avec le choix et la liberté d'Araminte. III. L'intention maternelle et le respect du choix de la fille peuvent se concilier."),
            S("Add evidence", text="For each part, note two scenes from your copy (act and scene) that you will refer to."),
        ]),
        H("Step 3: write the introduction"),
        KEY("Quatre temps de l'introduction", "Write them in this order, in four to six sentences.", cards=[
            ["L'amorce", "A general idea that leads to the subject: a fact, a link to society."],
            ["Le sujet", "State or rephrase the claim that you must discuss."],
            ["La problématique", "The question your essay answers."],
            ["L'annonce du plan", "The parts, with connectors: d'abord, ensuite, enfin."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a model introduction", intro="Write the introduction for the subject of worked example 1.", steps=[
            S("Amorce", text="« Dans toutes les sociétés, on attend des mères qu'elles veillent au bonheur de leurs filles. »"),
            S("Sujet", text="« Cette idée s'exprime dans l'affirmation selon laquelle une mère agit toujours dans l'intérêt de sa fille. Dans Les Fausses Confidences, comédie de Marivaux, Madame Argante cherche à orienter le mariage de sa fille Araminte. »"),
            S("Problématique", text="« Peut-on dire que Madame Argante défend réellement l'intérêt de sa fille ? »"),
            S("Annonce", text="« Nous verrons d'abord qu'elle agit selon ce qu'elle croit être le bien d'Araminte, puis que sa conception de l'intérêt contrarie la liberté de sa fille, avant de montrer que l'intention maternelle et le choix de la fille peuvent se concilier. »"),
        ]),
        H("Step 4: build the development"),
        T("Each paragraph has one idea. Use this sequence: **argument** (a sentence that states the idea), **exemple** (an exact reference to the work, with a short quotation or summary), **analyse** (what it proves), **transition** (a link to the next paragraph). Use **connecteurs logiques**: d'abord, de plus, en effet, par exemple (addition and example), mais, cependant, toutefois (contrast), donc, ainsi, par conséquent (consequence), en revanche (opposition)."),
        T("Write about literature in the **present tense** (« Madame Argante refuse... », not « refusa »). Use the passé composé or imparfait only for events before the story's present."),
        H("Step 5: the conclusion"),
        T("A conclusion has three moves: a **bilan** (a short summary of the argument), the **réponse** to the problématique, and an **ouverture** (a wider question or link)."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a model conclusion", intro="Write the conclusion for the same subject.", steps=[
            S("Bilan", text="« Au terme de cette analyse, il apparaît que Madame Argante agit selon ce qu'elle estime être l'intérêt de sa fille. »"),
            S("Réponse", text="« Mais cet intérêt, fondé sur le rang et la fortune, n'est pas celui qu'Araminte choisit : l'amour maternel n'est pas en cause, c'est sa manière de s'exercer. »"),
            S("Ouverture", text="« On peut se demander quelle place les familles d'aujourd'hui accordent à la parole des jeunes dans les grandes décisions. »"),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("No problématique", "A list of ideas on the topic is not a dissertation. Without a clear question, there is no argument. Always write the problématique and check your plan answers it."),
        MISTAKE("A prepared essay that ignores the subject", "Do not reproduce a memorised essay on the theme. The Board's report says the question was often not understood and the answers did not link to it. Use the subject's own words to build your plan."),
        MISTAKE("No examples from the text", "The Board's report says that where candidates did not use concrete facts from the text, scores were very low. After every argument, give a precise example from the work."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 2 and Paper 3 essays reward clear French, logical connectors and a visible argument. Plan for five minutes before you write. Check your own paper's instructions and time."),
        qz("What is the purpose of the problématique?",
           "To state the central question that the whole essay answers",
           [("To summarise the work from beginning to end", "A summary belongs in a résumé, not in a problématique."),
            ("To list all the quotations you know", "Quotations serve the argument but are not the problématique."),
            ("To give the author's biography", "Biography is secondary; the problématique is about the subject.")],
           0, "The problématique is the question around which the essay is organised, and the conclusion must answer it.", hint="What do all the parts of the essay answer?"),
        qz("Which sequence correctly describes a dialectical plan?",
           "Thèse, antithèse, synthèse",
           [("Synthèse, thèse, antithèse", "The synthèse comes last, after the two opposite positions."),
            ("Thèse, thèse, thèse", "Three parts that say the same thing do not form a dialectical plan."),
            ("Résumé, citation, biographie", "These are not argumentative parts.")],
           2, "A dialectical plan first supports the claim, then challenges it, and finally goes beyond both views.", hint="Which part resolves the opposition?"),
        PQ("Write a problématique and a two-part plan for the invented subject: « Baudelaire n'écrit que sur l'amour. Discutez à partir de deux poèmes de votre sélection. »", "Problématique: « Dans quelle mesure l'amour est-il le seul thème de la poésie de Baudelaire ? » Plan: I. L'amour est un thème important de ses poèmes (choose a poem of your selection and say what it shows). II. Mais d'autres thèmes élargissent la poésie : le spleen, la beauté, la ville, la mort (choose a second poem of your selection). Conclusion: love is one theme among others. Choose real titles and lines from your own selection."),
        PQ("Rewrite this weak paragraph start so that it follows argument-example-analysis: « Il y a beaucoup de choses dans la pièce. »", "Argument: « Madame Argante veut un mariage de rang pour sa fille. » Exemple: reference to a scene of your copy where she speaks of the Comte (act and scene). Analyse: « Ce choix montre qu'elle juge le bonheur de sa fille en fonction de la fortune et du titre. » Transition: « Mais Araminte ne voit pas les choses de la même manière. »"),
        PQ("Give three connecteurs logiques for each function: addition, opposition, consequence.", "Addition: de plus, en outre, d'ailleurs. Opposition: mais, cependant, toutefois (also en revanche, pourtant). Consequence: donc, ainsi, par conséquent (also c'est pourquoi, de ce fait). Use them to show the logic of your argument, not as decoration."),
        SUM(
            "A literary dissertation answers a question about literature with an organised argument and exact examples from your set texts.",
            "Analyse the subject, formulate the problématique, and choose a dialectical or thematic plan.",
            "The introduction has four moves: amorce, sujet, problématique, annonce du plan; the conclusion has bilan, réponse and ouverture.",
            "Use the present tense, logical connectors and a reference from the text in each paragraph.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Integrer les citations
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_DIS, topic="Intégrer les citations",
    title="Intégrer les citations : citer, paraphraser, commenter",
    slug="fru1-integrer-citations",
    minutes=22,
    summary="How to use evidence from a set text in a French essay: when to quote and when to paraphrase, how to integrate a quotation grammatically with « », […] and [ ], how to refer to act, chapter or line, and how to comment on it, practised on invented extracts.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish a quotation, a paraphrase and a reference, and choose among them",
        "Integrate a short quotation into a French sentence with correct punctuation, ellipsis and brackets",
        "Introduce, quote and then comment (citation, explication, lien) in a paragraph",
        "Avoid errors: quotations that are too long, inexact, ungrammatical or unexplained",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Citation", "Paraphrase", "Référence", "Guillemets français", "Points de suspension entre crochets", "Verbes introducteurs"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The Board's report says that in the weakest Paper 2 answers candidates were unable to support their analyses with concrete examples or facts from the text. A quotation is one of the strongest forms of concrete evidence: it shows the examiner that you have read the text closely. But a quotation can also hurt you if it is too long, misquoted or dropped into your essay without comment. This lesson shows how to use evidence well."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        D("A **citation** reproduces the exact words of the text, between guillemets français (« »). A **paraphrase** says the same idea in your own words, with a precise reference. A **référence** names the place: « acte I, scène 2 », « chapitre 10 », « vers 4 », or « dans le deuxième quatrain ».", title="Trois façons de s'appuyer sur le texte"),
        KEY("Quote or paraphrase?", "Choose according to the text and to your memory.", cards=[
            ["Citer", "When the exact words matter (a striking image, a key word). Keep it short: a few words to one or two lines."],
            ["Paraphraser", "When the idea matters more than the words, or when you cannot recall the exact wording."],
            ["Renvoyer", "When you point to an episode with a precise reference: « à l'acte II, Dubois prépare une confidence ». "],
        ]),
        T("If you are not sure of the exact words, **do not invent a quotation**. A wrong quotation shows that you did not read closely; a clear paraphrase with a precise reference is better. In your preparation, copy your best quotations into your fiche and learn them exactly."),
        H("Three ways to integrate a quotation"),
        T("Here is an invented extract for practice: « La cour était vide. Seul le vent jouait avec le papier que quelqu'un avait oublié. Il pensa que la liberté ressemblait à ce papier : légère, et perdue. »"),
        EX("Worked example 1: integrating the same words in three ways", intro="Use the invented extract above to prove that the narrator sees freedom as fragile.", steps=[
            S("Syntactic integration", text="« Pour le personnage, la liberté est « légère, et perdue ». » The fragment is part of the sentence and its gender (feminine) fits « liberté »."),
            S("Colon", text="« La comparaison est claire : « la liberté ressemblait à ce papier ». » The colon announces the quotation, which comes after a complete sentence."),
            S("Fragment", text="« Le papier « oublié » devient le symbole d'une liberté fragile. » A single word in guillemets is quoted exactly."),
            S("Comment", text="After each, explain: « Cette image montre que la liberté semble à la fois précieuse et menacée. »"),
        ]),
        T("In the steps above, the quotation marks inside a sentence that is itself between « » are only there to show you the example. In your own essay you will not nest guillemets: write the sentence normally and put only the author's words between « »."),
        H("Changing a quotation correctly"),
        KEY("Signes de la citation", "Three small marks to master.", cards=[
            ["Points de suspension entre crochets […]", "Show that you left out words from the original: « La cour était vide. […] Il pensa que la liberté ressemblait à ce papier »."],
            ["Crochets [ ]", "Show that you changed a word so that the sentence is grammatical: « [Le personnage] pensa que la liberté ressemblait à ce papier »."],
            ["Barre oblique /", "In poetry, show the end of a line: « Le fleuve emporte l'heure et le jour sans bruit, / Et mon cœur, immobile, attend la nuit. » (invented)"],
        ]),
        T("Do not change the original wording except with these marks. Keep the tense of the original inside the guillemets: if the extract has the passé simple or the imparfait, the quotation keeps it, while your own sentence around it is in the présent."),
        H("Introducing and commenting"),
        D("**Verbes introducteurs** announce the evidence: affirmer, souligner, révéler, suggérer, montrer, décrire, comparer, insister sur, laisser entendre. Use the **présent** for the author's or narrator's action: « L'auteur souligne... ».", title="Verbes introducteurs"),
        KEY("Citation, explication, lien", "A strong paragraph follows three moves around each quotation.", cards=[
            ["1. Introduire", "A sentence that states the idea and announces the evidence."],
            ["2. Citer", "The exact words, short, with a reference."],
            ["3. Commenter", "What the words show and how they prove your idea (figure, tense, tone, effect)."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: repairing a weak use of a quotation", intro="Weak version: « La cour était vide. Seul le vent jouait avec le papier que quelqu'un avait oublié. Il pensa que la liberté ressemblait à ce papier : légère, et perdue. C'est beau. » Rewrite it as an analysis.", steps=[
            S("Problem 1", text="The whole passage is copied, so there is no selection and no guillemets. Choose the key words."),
            S("Problem 2", text="« C'est beau » is not a comment. Say what the image shows and how."),
            S("Rewrite", text="« L'auteur souligne la fragilité de la liberté grâce à une comparaison : pour le personnage, elle « ressemblait à ce papier », « légère, et perdue ». Le choix d'un objet banal et abandonné suggère que la liberté peut disparaître sans bruit. »"),
            S("Check", text="Exact words in guillemets, a verb introducteur in the présent, and a comment on the effect of the comparison."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Dropping a quotation without a sentence around it", "A quotation standing alone in the middle of a paragraph is not an argument. Introduce it, quote briefly, then comment."),
        MISTAKE("Long quotations", "Copying several lines wastes time and shows less skill than selecting the words that prove your point. Keep your quotation to a few words or a line."),
        MISTAKE("Inventing or misquoting", "If you are not sure of the wording, do not guess. Paraphrase and give a reference. Wrong quotations can cost marks."),
        TIP("Exam link: in Paper 2 essays and in unseen-poem or passage analysis, the examiner looks for exact, short evidence and a comment. For Paper 3 essays, the same method applies to facts and examples. Check the instructions on your own paper."),
        qz("Which sentence integrates the quotation correctly?",
           "Le personnage voit la liberté comme « légère, et perdue ».",
           [("Le personnage voit la liberté comme. « Légère, et perdue »", "The quotation has been separated from the sentence by a full stop, breaking the grammar."),
            ("Légère, et perdue, c'est ce que le personnage voit.", "There are no guillemets, so the borrowed words are not marked as a quotation."),
            ("« Le personnage voit la liberté comme légère, et perdue », ce qui est beau.", "The whole sentence is put in guillemets, though only the last words are the author's.")],
           0, "The quotation is placed in guillemets and fits the grammar of the sentence.", hint="Which version keeps the grammar and marks only the author's words?"),
        qz("What does […] mean inside a quotation?",
           "Words of the original have been left out",
           [("The author hesitated", "The author's own hesitation would be shown by the original punctuation, not by […]."),
            ("The quotation continues on the next page", "It does not show page breaks."),
            ("The student changed a word", "That is shown by single words in [ ], not by […].")],
           2, "The ellipsis between brackets shows an omission by the person quoting.", hint="Think of cutting."),
        PQ("Rewrite correctly using the invented extract: « Il pensa que la liberté ressemblait à ce papier » placed inside your sentence about the narrator.", "« Le narrateur compare la liberté à un papier oublié : pour lui, elle « ressemblait à ce papier ». » (Or with brackets: « [Le personnage] pensa que la liberté ressemblait à ce papier ».) The tense of the quotation is kept inside the guillemets and the introduction is in the présent."),
        PQ("Write two sentences: one that quotes and one that paraphrases the same idea from a text you have studied. Use placeholders for the actual words.", "Quotation: « Le narrateur affirme que [exact words from your copy] (chapitre X). » Paraphrase: « Le narrateur explique que [the idea in your own words] (chapitre X). » In both, give the reference. Use the quotation if you can reproduce it exactly, the paraphrase if you cannot."),
        PQ("Give three verbes introducteurs in the présent and say what each suggests about the author's action.", "Souligner: the author draws attention to an idea. Suggérer: the author does not say it directly but lets the reader understand. Révéler: the author uncovers something hidden about a character or situation. (Others: montrer, comparer, insister sur.)"),
        SUM(
            "Use citations for exact words, paraphrase when the idea matters more than the wording, and a précis reference in either case.",
            "Quote briefly, between « », integrated into your sentence; use […] for omissions, [ ] for changes and / for line breaks in poetry.",
            "Introduce the quotation with a verbe introducteur in the présent and always add a comment.",
            "Never invent or guess a quotation: a wrong quotation is worse than a clear paraphrase.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. Le commentaire compose
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_DIS, topic="Le commentaire composé",
    title="Le commentaire composé : lire, choisir des axes, rédiger",
    slug="fru1-commentaire-compose",
    minutes=25,
    summary="A complete method for the commentaire composé, applied to an invented eight-line poem: reading grid, projet de lecture, three axes, introduction, a developed paragraph and a conclusion, with the points the Board's report expects for an unseen poem.",
    objectives=[
        "Read a poem or prose passage methodically and fill a grid of structure, tenses, lexical fields and figures",
        "Formulate a projet de lecture and choose two or three axes",
        "Write the introduction, a developed paragraph and the conclusion of a commentaire composé in French",
        "Use the points the Board's report expects for the unseen poem: external and internal structure, role of tenses, lexical field, tone",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Commentaire composé", "Projet de lecture", "Axe de lecture", "Champ lexical", "Structure externe et interne", "Procédé et effet"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The unseen poem was the most popular question in Paper 2 in the Board's 2023 report: most candidates who answered Paper 2 chose it, yet only about one in five reached the pass mark. The report also says what examiners were looking for: the external structure (rhymes, lines, strophes), the internal structure (what each strophe says), the role of the tenses, a lexical field that shows whether the poet is optimistic or pessimistic, the advice hidden in a line, and a clear statement of what the poem says. That is exactly what a good commentaire composé does."),
        T("This lesson gives you the method. We use an **original invented poem** so that you can see every step."),
        D("Le **commentaire composé** est l'étude organisée d'un texte court : on rassemble les observations en quelques **axes** (idées directrices) qui répondent à un **projet de lecture** (ce que le texte veut dire ou faire). Il diffère de l'explication linéaire, qui suit le texte ligne par ligne.", title="Définition"),
        IMG("alfru1-commentaire-method.svg", alt="A four-step flow: Lire et relever, Projet de lecture, Axes, Rédiger. Each step is a box with an arrow to the next.", caption="Figure 1. The four steps of a commentaire composé."),
        H("Step 1: read and collect"),
        T("Here is the invented poem « Le port au soir »:"),
        CODE("Le jour s'éteint sur le vieux port,\nOù dorment les barques de bois ;\nLes mâts se courbent vers le nord,\nEt moi, je me tais devant toi.\n\nTu as pris tant de nos départs,\nTant de mains levées sur le quai,\nQue je garde, au fond du regard,\nComme un trésor jamais troqué.", "text", title="Le port au soir (poème inventé pour l'exercice)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: the reading grid", intro="Fill the grid for « Le port au soir ».", steps=[
            S("Structure externe", text="Two quatrains of eight syllables (octosyllabes) with crossed rhymes: port / bois / nord / toi (ABAB), départs / quai / regard / troqué (CDCD). All the rhymes are masculine."),
            S("Structure interne", text="Strophe 1: the scene of the port at sundown, and the speaker's silence. Strophe 2: the speaker addresses the sea and says what he keeps of the departures."),
            S("Temps", text="Présent in strophe 1 (s'éteint, dorment, se courbent, me tais): a quiet scene. Passé composé in line 5 (« tu as pris »): the sea has taken departures in the past. Présent again in « je garde »: the memory remains now."),
            S("Champs lexicaux", text="Sea and port (port, barques, mâts, quai, nord); silence (se tais, dorment); loss and memory (départs, regard, troqué)."),
            S("Figures", text="Apostrophe (tu, toi: the speaker speaks to the sea), personification (« dorment les barques »), comparison (« comme un trésor »), antithesis between « pris » and « garde », hyperbole (« tant de »)."),
            S("Ton", text="Calm, respectful, melancholic but consoled: not simply pessimistic."),
        ]),
        H("Step 2: projet de lecture and axes"),
        T("The **projet de lecture** is one question that unites your observations: « Comment le poème transforme-t-il un paysage de port au crépuscule en méditation sur la perte et le souvenir ? » Each **axe** is a sentence that answers part of that question. Choose two or three axes and give each a title that states an idea, not a topic. The axes must not repeat each other."),
        KEY("Trois axes pour « Le port au soir »", "Axes built from the grid.", cards=[
            ["Axe 1", "Un décor de fin de jour, calme et silencieux (champ lexical du port, présent, personnification)."],
            ["Axe 2", "Une adresse intime à la mer (apostrophe, « tu » et « moi », passé composé)."],
            ["Axe 3", "De la perte au trésor : la consolation du souvenir (antithèse « pris » et « garde », comparaison, « jamais troqué »)."],
        ]),
        H("Step 3: the introduction"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a model introduction", intro="Write the introduction for « Le port au soir ».", steps=[
            S("Présentation du texte", text="« *Le port au soir* est un poème de huit vers, formé de deux quatrains en octosyllabes (texte inventé pour l'exercice). »"),
            S("Situation et thème", text="« Le poète s'y adresse à un « toi » qui semble être la mer, au moment où le jour finit. »"),
            S("Projet de lecture", text="« Comment le poème transforme-t-il un paysage de port au crépuscule en méditation sur la perte et le souvenir ? »"),
            S("Annonce des axes", text="« Nous verrons d'abord un décor calme et silencieux, puis une adresse intime à la mer, enfin comment la perte devient un trésor. »"),
        ]),
        H("Step 4: the development"),
        T("Each axis becomes a paragraph or two. Use the cycle: **idée** (the axis stated), **procédé** (the device, the tense or the word), **citation** (short, exact), **effet** (what it does and why). Avoid listing figures without effects."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a developed paragraph (axe 3)", intro="Write the paragraph for the third axis.", steps=[
            S("Idée", text="« Le poème fait de la perte une richesse intérieure. »"),
            S("Procédé et citation", text="« Le poète oppose deux verbes : la mer « a pris » les départs, mais lui « garde » leur image « au fond du regard ». »"),
            S("Procédé et citation", text="« La comparaison « comme un trésor » donne une valeur précieuse à ce souvenir, et l'adverbe « jamais » dans « jamais troqué » en fait une possession définitive. »"),
            S("Effet", text="« Le ton, d'abord mélancolique, devient consolé : la perte n'est pas effacée, mais transformée en trésor. »"),
        ]),
        H("Step 5: the conclusion"),
        EX("Worked example 4: a model conclusion", intro="Write the conclusion.", steps=[
            S("Bilan", text="« Le poème construit d'abord un décor silencieux, puis un dialogue avec la mer, enfin une consolation. »"),
            S("Réponse au projet", text="« La perte devient ainsi un trésor intérieur, ce qui donne au poème un ton plus apaisé que triste. »"),
            S("Ouverture", text="« On pourrait comparer ce regard apaisé sur le souvenir avec celui d'autres poèmes de votre sélection. »"),
        ]),
        KEY("Pour un poème inconnu", "The points the Board's report expects, in a checklist.", cards=[
            ["Structure externe", "Strophes, lines, syllables, rhymes."],
            ["Structure interne", "What each strophe says and how the poem moves."],
            ["Temps verbaux", "What does each tense do (past, present, future)?"],
            ["Champ lexical", "Which words dominate? Optimistic or pessimistic?"],
            ["Message", "What does the poet say or advise?"],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Paraphrasing instead of analysing", "Retelling each strophe is not a commentary. For each observation, name the device, give the words and say what it does."),
        MISTAKE("A list of figures", "Do not write « il y a une comparaison, une personnification, une antithèse » and stop. Group them under an axis and explain their effect."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the form", "The structure externe belongs in the commentary. A poem's rhymes and strophes help to show its movement."),
        TIP("Exam link: the unseen poem is a common choice in Paper 2, and the Board's report asks teachers to also teach the selected poems, because too many only prepare for the unseen question. Use this method on both. Check your own paper's instructions."),
        qz("In « Le port au soir », what is the role of the passé composé in « Tu as pris tant de nos départs » ?",
           "It places the sea's taking of departures in the past, in contrast with the present scene",
           [("It announces the poet's death", "The line speaks of departures already taken, not of the poet's death."),
            ("It is the future tense", "The passé composé is a past tense."),
            ("It has no effect", "Each tense creates an effect; here, a contrast between past losses and the present.")],
           0, "The passé composé looks back to past departures while the present tense describes the evening scene and the memory the poet keeps.", hint="Past or future?"),
        qz("Which is the best title for an axe?",
           "De la perte au trésor : la consolation du souvenir",
           [("Les figures de style", "A topic, not an idea: it does not say what the figures show."),
            ("Résumé du poème", "A summary is not an axe."),
            ("Le poète et sa vie", "Biography is not the focus of a commentaire.")],
           2, "A good axis title states an idea that the observations support.", hint="Which title makes a claim?"),
        PQ("Give the rhyme scheme of the first quatrain of « Le port au soir » (port / bois / nord / toi) and say whether the rhymes are masculine or feminine.", "ABAB (port and nord rhyme, bois and toi rhyme): crossed rhymes (rimes croisées). All four end in a sounded syllable, so they are masculine."),
        PQ("Write two axes for this invented quatrain: « La pluie chante sur le toit, / Les enfants se taisent, sans voix ; / Puis le tonnerre s'en va, / Et le soleil revient déjà. »", "Axe 1: l'orage comme spectacle sonore et apaisant (la pluie « chante », les enfants écoutent en silence). Axe 2: le retour rapide du calme (« puis », « s'en va », « déjà »: succession of present tense verbs and an adverb that stresses how quickly the storm passes). Link each axis to words from the quatrain, the tenses (all in the présent) and the effect: a feeling of wonder and then relief."),
        PQ("Write a projet de lecture for « Le port au soir » different from the one in the lesson, and say if it works with the three axes.", "Possible projet: « Comment le poète fait-il du silence le langage de l'émotion ? » It works partly with axe 1 (a silent scene) and axe 2 (he speaks to the sea but says only that he keeps something), but less with axe 3 on consolation. So the best projet is the one that unites all the axes: transformation of a scene into a meditation on loss and memory."),
        SUM(
            "The commentaire composé organises your observations in two or three axes that answer one projet de lecture, unlike the linear explication.",
            "Read with a grid: structure externe and interne, tenses, lexical fields, figures, tone.",
            "Write an introduction, paragraphs of idée-procédé-citation-effet, and a conclusion that answers the projet.",
            "Follow the Board's checklist for the unseen poem, and practise the method on the selected poems too.",
        ),
    ],
))
